Z||000007||Raydar||09-27-2001||09:03 PM||||Dennis,<P>Don't remove the cold start valve. Just disable it. Disconnect the wires, if you can. (Don't know. Never had mine apart.)<P>If you can get the engine to start only by holding the gas to the floor, it sounds like it is flooding. <BR>Can you smell a lot of gas? Does it smoke like hell when it starts?<P>The reason I'm asking all this stuff, is a couple of years ago I went to look at a Formula that a guy had for sale. In order to get it to crank, I had to hold the gas flat on the floor while I was cranking it. Eventually it would chug to life, once it cleared out all the gas. Don't think it would even idle. He also said it needed a new cat. That figured, with as much gas as was being poured through it.<P>Having said all that, I figured that all that gas had to be coming from <I>somewhere</I>. Either the cold start valve or it's sensor had gone berzerk, or the injectors were leaking. Since the injectors are controlled by the ECM, I figured they wouldn't cause a problem <I>this</I> bad.<P>I suppose the fuel regulator <I>could</I> be causing it too, but I don't see how. <P>Is it possible that when you put the plenum back on, that you pinched one of the wires going to the cold start valve? Might be causing the cold start valve to run "wide open"?<P>I don't have the answers, but from what you're telling me, it sure sounds like it's flooding -in a big way. That, and the folks at the dealer said there was gas in the oil.  <P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
