Z||000011||Raydar||09-28-2001||11:17 AM||||There is a schrader valve on the fuel line. It's just below the MAP sensor. There is probably a metal cap on it. It'll probably squirt when you press the valve. Watch your eyes.||||reg||1
Z||000012||dennis_6||09-28-2001||12:25 PM||||Well the system had 0 pressure in it.  No mess at all there was fuel in the rail though.  <BR>My #6 cylinder had a spark plug screwed to far into the head from the previous owner.  No way to get it out so I just assumed it would not hurt anything.  <P>Well that same cylinder had fuel runnig out of the injector hole.  So I figure the cylinder was not firing at all thus causing the engine to flood.  The fuel rail had a mismatched injector set. 4 stock and 2 aftermarket injectors.  <P>What would you do with this engine?  Should I drain the oil and let it sit and try to pull the engine later and put a new head on the engine or should I just find a replacement.  <P>I am getting a parts car not to far from now, but supposedly it just needs a clutch and some minor work.  I don't know if I will be able to tear it apart if it looks good. <P>So what do you guys think?  Apreciate everyone that has helped.||||reg||1
Z||000013||dennis_6||09-28-2001||06:26 PM||||The injector holes in the block had a lot of crud in them I cleaned out what I could.  <P>Now the engine does not even try to start.  I think the crud is blocking the fuel spray or something.  Note I did remove the wire for the number 6 cylinder, but I don't think this would shut down the fuel system.  <P>Should I just replace the head with the bad plug or should I remove the engine and tear it apart and try to find any garbage that found its way into the cylinder?||||reg||5
Z||000014||JetroGT||09-28-2001||06:48 PM||||I would pull the fuel pump fuse, and unplug the coil. Then crank the engine a few times to get the gas out of the engine. Get some new injectors, check the vacume hose harness and reassemble it. <BR>BTW, you probably alaready know but new injectors are expensive.||||reg||1
Z||000015||dennis_6||09-28-2001||07:39 PM||||Raydar- I have 16 fuel injectors, and two fuel rails I should have a good set if I just knew how to test them.<P>Does anyone know what type of volatage the fuel injectors operate off of?||||reg||1
Z||000016||JetroGT||09-29-2001||05:05 AM||||I think you just measure the resistance.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Raydar||09-29-2001||07:27 AM||||I've also heard that you check the resistance, but I don't know what the value is supposed to be.<BR>There are people who can test injectors for leaks/correct spray pattern. Don't know any names, though. Way cheaper than buying new ones.<P>If one of the cylinders was full of gas, I'm guessing the injector that fed that cylinder was leaking. Maybe to the point that none of the other cylinders was getting <I>enough</I> gas. <BR>Pure speculation, though.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000018||dennis_6||09-29-2001||07:35 PM||||I really think I am just going to replace the engine and see if it is worth rebuilding.<BR>That could not hurt.  Just going to have to save my pennies.  Thanks everyone||||reg||1
